## std.bench — runtime allocation counters + benchmark harness.
##
## Real impl (v0.1): the counters are backed by the always-emitted
## `__zag_bench_*` preamble family (src/codegen/core.zig). Every
## `new` / `alloc` site emits `__zag_bench_alloc(@sizeOf(T))` and
## the matching destroy/free sites emit `__zag_bench_free(<size>)`,
## so `Counters.snapshot()` reports live heap bytes, cumulative
## allocated bytes, and the allocation count — without touching the
## allocator itself (no logging-allocator swap, no per-site call
## overhead beyond two integer adds).
##
## ── The leak check ────────────────────────────────────────────────
## `bytes_live` is a LEDGER, so "did I leak?" is a comparison rather
## than a judgement call:
##
##     let before: usize = Counters.snapshot().bytes_live;
##     ... allocate, use, free ...
##     assert(Counters.snapshot().bytes_live == before);
##
## That only works if every allocation path is accounted, so the
## charges live in the PRIMITIVES rather than at call sites: the raw
## tier is charged inside std.mem (alloc_raw charges n, realloc_raw
## charges the delta, release discharges cap — see lib/std/mem.zag
## §"Allocation accounting"), and the `new` / `free` keyword paths
## charge and discharge at the emit. A container that is never
## released therefore shows its CAPACITY as live bytes; a container
## released with the wrong `cap` leaves a residue equal to the
## difference.
##
## Worked example + the gated assertions: examples/memory/leak_check.zag.
## The compile-time leak warning is the other half of the story: the
## escape analysis flags a `new` / `alloc` / `alloc_raw` site that
## never escapes its function and is never freed (docs/manual/20).
##
## Pre-migration state: the stub returned zeros. Per-line benchmark
## scaffolding (timing + per-source-line counters) is a follow-up.

pub struct Counters {
    bytes_live: usize,
    bytes_total: usize,
    allocations: usize,
}

impl Counters {
    pub fun snapshot() -> Counters {
        return Counters {
            bytes_live: __zag_bench_live(),
            bytes_total: __zag_bench_total(),
            allocations: __zag_bench_allocs(),
        };
    }
}
